==============================================================================
C2  Integer points of the multiplicative exceptional set on both sheets
==============================================================================
x in Bad_b iff every prefix of the T_b-parity sequence of x has 3^a > 2^j.  For an integer whose
orbit enters a cycle, membership is decided by following the orbit one full period past the entry
(the cycle multiplier then repeats), so the check below is exact.
  (prefilter: n mod 2^16 in Bad_16 leaves 322572 resp. 322569 candidates below 10^7)
  Bad_- cap [1, 10^7] = [1, 5, 17]      (= -(Bad_+ cap [-10^7, -1]) by negation)
  Bad_+ cap [1, 10^7] = []
  Hence (FINITE-EXACT): Bad_+ cap [-10^7, 10^7] = {-17, -5, -1}  and  Bad_- cap [-10^7, 10^7] = {1, 5, 17}.
  The statement 'Bad cap Z = the points of least |x| on the three expanding integer cycles' is sheet-symmetric;
  only the SIGN of those three integers tells the sheets apart.

==============================================================================
C3  DEFECT controls: planted density-zero modifications invisible to the type data
==============================================================================
Control P1 (one trit).  T1(n) = 2n if n = 3*2^m (m >= 1), T1(n) = T(n) otherwise.
  * S1 = {3*2^m : m >= 1} is T1-invariant and T1^-1(S1) is inside S1 (T^-1 of a multiple of 3 is its
    double only), so every orbit starting outside S1 is its T-orbit; S1 itself is a divergent orbit.
  * T1(3*2^m) - T(3*2^m) = 9*2^(m-1): at level k (types mod 3*2^k) the type map of T1 equals that
    of T except at the k-1 integers 3*2^m, m < k.  #S1 cap [1,X] = floor(log2(X/3)).
  verified: discrepancy 9*2^(m-1), agreement mod 3*2^(k-1) for all k <= m (m < 39).

Control P2 (all 2- and 3-adic levels).  s_1 = 12 and s_(i+1) = s_i/2 + 6^i k_i, with the least k_i
  making 2^(i+2) | s_(i+1) and s_(i+1) > s_i^2.  T2(s_i) = s_(i+1); T2 = T elsewhere.
  s_1..s_4 = [12, 168, 28272, 799306368]; s_5 has 18 digits, s_7 has 72 digits.
  verified: T2(s_i) = T(s_i) mod 6^i (so the discrepancy tends to 0 in Z_2 AND in Z_3), s_(i+1) > s_i^2,
  3 | s_i, and the D-chains {s_i 2^j} are pairwise disjoint.  The affected set is the union of the
  D-chains of the s_i: O(log X * log log X) integers up to X.  T2 has the divergent orbit s_1, s_2, ...
  Affected integers <= 10^6 (both controls together): 37.  Every statement about T that
  tolerates exceptions on a density-zero set of starting points (densities, log-densities, 'almost all',
  tree-count lower bounds, residue-class statements with density-zero exceptions, and the one-step type
  maps F_k up to finitely many exceptions per level) holds verbatim for T1 and T2; Collatz fails for both.

==============================================================================
C4  Z-Collatz on [-N, N]: the sheet-symmetric statement that contains Collatz
==============================================================================
  T_+ on [-10^6, 10^6]: 0 is fixed; every positive integer reaches {1,2}; every negative integer x
  reaches -1, {-5,-7,-10} or {-17,...,-34} (checked as T_-(-x) = -T_+(x) on [1, 10^6]).
  The five integer cycles of T_+ on [-10^6, 10^6], with parity words read from the listed point:
    0              word 0            p= 1 a=0  contracting zero
    {1,2}          word 10           p= 2 a=1  contracting positive
    {-1}           word 1            p= 1 a=1  expanding   negative
    {-5,-7,-10}    word 110          p= 3 a=2  expanding   negative
    {-17,...}      word 11110111000  p=11 a=7  expanding   negative
  (i) every integer orbit in [-10^6, 10^6] is bounded; (ii) the contracting integer cycles are exactly
  0 and {1,2}.  Both are sheet-symmetric statements; the sign law (contracting <=> positive on the plus
  sheet) turns them into Collatz (Theorem 15 of the note).
  Z-Collatz (all integers, both signs, five cycles named by their parity words) is invariant under
  negation: it is the same statement for T_+ and for T_-.  Z-Collatz plus the sign check '{1,2} is the
  only one of the five cycles inside Z_{>0}' implies Collatz (the check is true for T_+ and false for T_-,
  whose positive cycles are 1, 5, 17).  Z-Collatz is strictly stronger than Collatz: it contains the
  3n-1 conjecture on the positive integers.

