  Exact: sum_a N_d(a) = 4^d over Z/3^d, so the Haar mean is (4/3)^d.  Per-type means (m0 = 1 on 3Z):
  m1_d = m2_(d-1) (the D-child of a = 1 is = 2 mod 3), m2_d = m1_(d-1) + (4/3)^(d-1) (the E-child is Haar-
  uniform on Z_3); ratio m2/m1 -> 4/3, the Perron vector of A_6.
  (recursion checked exactly: (m0 + m1_d + m2_d)/3 = (4/3)^d for d <= 14)
  Bivariate identity sum_a N_(d,e)(a) = C(d,e) 3^(d-e), i.e. E[N_(d,e)] = C(d,e) 3^-e, checked d <= 9;
  generating function E[sum_(d,e) N_(d,e) z^d u^e] = 1/(1 - z(1 + u/3)).
  The minimum over 3-adic units grows by factors 1.250, 1.400, 1.286, 1.389, 1.280, 1.344 (last six):
  min_d^(1/d) at d = 14: 1.3082 (vs 4/3 = 1.3333).  The spread is the object of Wirsching's
  'heuristic principle' (individual counts comparable to the mean); the automaton computes it exactly
  level by level but does not bound it uniformly.  (The minimising unit classes are those of the small
  integers 5, 7, 25, 34: thin trees are a property of specific 3-adic points, visible level by level.)

==============================================================================
K2  The Haar-averaged size series: poles at s = 1, 2 and the reciprocal AM-GM gap
==============================================================================
A depth-d predecessor with e E-steps has size ~ a 2^d/3^e (carry ignored).  Haar average over a in Z_3:
  F(s) = sum_(d,e) C(d,e) 3^-e (2^d/3^e)^-s = 1/(1 - g(s)),   g(s) = 2^-s + (3/2)^s/3.
  g(1) = 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 (arithmetic mean of the step factors 1/2, 3/2 is 1);  g(2) = 1/4 + 3/4 = 1;
  g < 1 exactly on (1, 2).  -g'(1) = (1/2) log(4/3) = log(2/sqrt 3) = 0.143841, the Collatz drift.
  Residue of F at s = 1:  R = 1/log(2/sqrt 3) = 6.952119.  (No other pole on Re s = 1: |g(1+it)| = 1
  would need 2^-it = (3/2)^it = 1, impossible for t != 0.)  By Wiener-Ikehara the averaged number of
  predecessors of relative size <= X is ~ R X, and in each dyadic window [2^n, 2^(n+1)) it is ~ R 2^n.
  Per-type residues (root uniform on a class mod 3): a = 0: 0 (the tree is the D-chain);
  a = 1 mod 3: 6.95212 = R;  a = 2 mod 3: 13.90424 = 2R.  Average over Z_3: (0 + R + 2R)/3 = R.
  Reading: orbits visit 2 mod 3 twice as often as 1 mod 3 (stationary law 2/3, 1/3 after the first odd
  step), and the visit rate at size y is 1/(y log(2/sqrt3)): renewal theory with the drift as mean step.
  Dyadic window means M_n = sum over n <= d - e log2 3 < n+1 of C(d,e) 3^-e (d < 6000):
   n  :      0       5      10      15      20      25      30      35      40
  M/2^n:  5.989   6.958   6.970   6.968   6.976   6.980   6.978   6.972   6.967
  mean of M_n/2^n over n = 30..40: 6.9721   (R = 6.9521)
  cumulative A(2^n)/2^n (Wiener-Ikehara predicts -> R): n=20: 6.9724, n=40: 6.9688, n=60: 6.9556, n=80: 6.9431
  The ratio oscillates slowly around R (the non-lattice renewal theorem gives convergence without a
  rate; the near-lattice structure comes from the convergents 19/12, 84/53, ... of log2 3).
  Same series for the shortcut qx+1 map (branch probability 1/q): g_q(s) = 2^-s (1 + q^(s-1)).
    q = 3: roots of g_q = 1 at s = [1.0, 2.0]; tree-count exponent = 1.0
    q = 5: roots of g_q = 1 at s = [0.650919, 1.0]; tree-count exponent = 0.650919
    q = 7: roots of g_q = 1 at s = [0.373501, 1.0]; tree-count exponent = 0.373501
    q = 9: roots of g_q = 1 at s = [0.258108, 1.0]; tree-count exponent = 0.258108
  s = 1 is always a root (arithmetic mean of 1/2 and q/2 is (q+1)/4, and the s = 1 identity reads
  1/2 + (1/q)(q/2) = 1).  For q = 3 the other root is 2 > 1, so the averaged tree has POSITIVE DENSITY;
  for q >= 5 the other root is < 1 (0.651 for q = 5): averaged trees have density zero.  The automaton
  sees DRIFT exactly.  It is blind to the sheet: g does not contain the offset b.

