  mod 192, (I): 24 classes = 3 lifts of [7, 15, 27, 31, 39, 47, 59, 63] mod 64
  mod 192, (II): 14 classes [7, 27, 31, 39, 63, 91, 103, 111, 123, 127, 135, 159, 175, 187]
   all begin with two odd steps and are 0 or 1 mod 3.  The class 191 = -1 is DECIDED in (II): for n >= 1
   in it, E(n) = (2n-1)/3 < n.  The 3-adic/2-adic point -1 itself is E's fixed point (E(-1) = -1), where
   the multiplier 2/3 never becomes descent: the class is decided, the hostile point is not.
  Reading: the mod-3 refinement removes a fraction of the undecided classes (about 45-50% for (II), with
  the ratio drifting slowly, and about 60% for (III) at k <= 18), never the exponent: log2(count)/k
  creeps up towards h(log_3 2) = 0.9500 in every column.  PROVED for (II) in the note (shifted barrier);
  (III) is FINITE-EXACT only.

==============================================================================
K5  Rational periodic points: sign, integrality, and which lie in Bad
==============================================================================
A primitive parity word w of length p with a odd steps has the unique 2-adic periodic point
x_w = c_w/(2^p - 3^a) (plus sheet; c_w > 0).  Sign(x_w) = sign(2^p - 3^a) [sign law]; x_w is an integer
iff (2^p - 3^a) | c_w, a congruence modulo a number PRIME TO 6, invisible to the 3*2^k tower.
  integer periodic points for p <= 18 (all primitive words): [-136, -91, -82, -68, -61, -55, -41, -37, -34, -25, -17, -10, -7, -5, -1, 0, 1, 2]
  = {0}, {1,2}, {-1}, {-5,-7,-10}, {-17,...,-136}: positive ones only on contracting words (2^p > 3^a),
  negative ones only on expanding words.
  periodic points lying in Bad (every prefix of the period above the line): integer ones [-17, -5, -1];
   count of rational ones per period p: p=1: 1, p=2: 0, p=3: 1, p=4: 2, p=5: 3, p=6: 6, p=7: 12, p=8: 16, p=9: 36, p=10: 60, p=11: 127, p=12: 216, p=13: 366, p=14: 721, p=15: 1290, p=16: 2095, p=17: 4227, p=18: 7451
  All of them are negative.  Under x -> -x they are the positive rational cycle points of 3x-1 that lie
  in Bad_-, among them the minima 1, 5, 17 of its three integer cycles.

==============================================================================
K6  The 16-point trap of E_{6 mod 8} is the negative image of the 3n-1 cycles
==============================================================================
  -F = [1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10, 14, 16, 20, 29, 32, 43, 64, 86, 128]
  is closed under the minus-sheet relaxation (x odd -> 3x-1; x even -> x/2, and 3x-1 also at x = 2 mod 8),
  equals the orbit of 1 there, and contains the 3n-1 cycles 1 -> 2 -> 1 and 5 -> 14 -> 7 -> 20 -> 10 -> 5
  (C-form), glued by the excursions at 2 and 10.  Its rate 2/3 is the rate of the 3n-1 cycle {5,7,10}
  (2 odd steps, 3 halvings; multiplier 9/8).  So the trap that refutes HYP-9121 is SHEET, seen from
  inside the plus sheet: the hostile neighbourhood of -1 is the mirror image of 3n-1's small cycles.
  In the full relaxation E (3x+1 allowed at every even) the orbit of -1 also meets the third cycle:
  -1 -> -2 -> -5 -> -14 -> -41 (3x+1 at the even -14), and -41 lies on the C-form -17 cycle.
  So all three 3n-1 cycles sit, mirrored, inside the plus sheet's hostile neighbourhood of -1.

==============================================================================
K7  The renewal identity for basins and its neutral main term
==============================================================================
For any set R with T^-1(R) = R (a basin, or a union of basins):  R = D(R) disjoint-union E(R cap G),
G = guard class (2 mod 3 plus, 1 mod 3 minus).  Hence, exactly, for every X:
   N_R(X) = N_R(X/2) + #{n in R cap G : E(n) <= X}.
  verified exactly for the plus basin of {1,2} and each of the three minus basins, X in {10^3,
  12345, 2*10^5, 6*10^5}.  Main terms: if R has density delta, equidistributed mod 3 (as the census
  shows for all four basins), then delta = delta/2 + (3/2)(delta/3) = delta for EVERY delta.
  The equation is neutral because the arithmetic mean of the step factors is 1: the automaton's
  renewal structure cannot fix a basin's density.  Density 1 (plus) versus 0.327/0.3245/0.348 (minus)
  is boundary data from the small numbers, i.e. non-residue information.

